<article class="env-lemma" id="0C7I">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C7I">Lemma <span data-tag="0C7I">55.4.2</span>.</a> Let $n, m_ i, a_{ij}, w_ i, g_ i$ be a numerical type $T$. The Picard group of $T$ is a finitely generated abelian group of rank $1$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $n = 1$, then $A = (a_{ij})$ is the zero matrix and the result is clear. For $n > 1$ the matrix $A$ has rank $n - 1$ by either Lemma <a href="/tag/0C5V" data-tag="0C5V">55.2.2</a> or Lemma <a href="/tag/0C5X" data-tag="0C5X">55.2.3</a>. Of course the rank is not affected by scaling the rows by $1/w_ i$. This proves the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>